From 6e5c6da47d05255463df72f5d5dfd1ed3321f2db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:22:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Send a sign-in request again when Apple answers too many
 requests

Apple's sign-in server, GrandSlam, answers a request with HTTP 429, Too
Many Requests, now and then, and the same request gets past it when it
goes again. The Cartouche Installer signs in through isideload 0.5.2,
which sends the request again, up to 10 times (err_429_retries), for
the sign-in's two requests and its app tokens; never for a verification
code, nor for the developer services. SideSign stopped at the first
429: Cartouche 0.11.2 showed Apple's page, read as an answer, as
"SideSign.ServerError 1" in 39 sign-ins of 17 copies, on the first
request or right after a verification code was checked, and 13 of those
copies got through at a later sign-in. Since patch 0016, Cartouche
waits 15 minutes before the next one.

The sign-in's requests to GrandSlam (sendAuthenticationRequest) now go
again while Apple answers 429, a second apart, 10 times at most
(GrandSlamResend). Still refused the eleventh time, a request gives
Apple's 429 as before (ServerError.httpStatus, patch 0016), and
Cartouche waits. The requests of the verification code and those of
the developer services go once, as the Installer's do.

Each request sent again counts in the outcome Cartouche reads
(cartouche.outcome, patch 0014) as resent: SignInOperation tells
CartoucheOutcome through GrandSlamResend.resent, for Cartouche's
measurements to say how many a sign-in needed, and whether any passed.
---
